window.docContents[35].resolve({"/UD-V___-encapsulation/Inheritance/#Core-C______--UD-V___-encapsulation--Inheritance":{"contents":"A pointer to a derived class is accepted where a pointer to its base is expected, in declarations, assignments, arguments, returns, comparisons and the branches of ?:. It is the only conversion between class types, and the other direction is a type error.\n\nA virtual method is chosen by the class tag  of the receiver, the nearest declaration in the chain of , so a call through a base pointer reaches the override of the derived class. A non virtual method is chosen by the static class . A derived class redefines only a method the base declares virtual, and marks it override, so the method reached from  and the one reached from  coincide for every non virtual method. Redefining a non virtual method, marking override without a virtual method in a base, or changing the signature is a type error.\n\nFor every well typed program, the method a call resolves to by the static class of the receiver and the method it resolves to by the class tag coincide whenever the method is not virtual. The nearest declaration of a non virtual  from  is also the nearest from , because no class between  and  redeclares . There is no Lean proof, the property follows from the rule T-Class by inspection.\n\nThe command delete e applies to a pointer to a class or to a vector. On an object it runs the destructors of the chain from the tag up to the root, each with this bound to the object, and removes the record and the field locations from . A second delete, a delete through a pointer to a base without a virtual destructor in its chain, and any later access are error, the three cases C++17 leaves undefined. delete nullptr does nothing, as in C++.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009UD V, encapsulation","header":"6.3. Inheritance","id":"/UD-V___-encapsulation/Inheritance/#Core-C______--UD-V___-encapsulation--Inheritance"}});